# Target C-II — bridge-deduplicated long-side contact concentration

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Composes:** main `target_c_outer_route_direction_concentration_2026-09-14.md` with the audited proof-only bridge-subtracted outer-face isoperimetry theorem  
**Status:** proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current long-side physicalization converts long loaded-hull side mass into
near-tangent outer-route edge occurrences and then divides by two because a physical
outer-face edge may occur twice.  In the full Type-II positive core that factor-two
loss is far too large: an outer-face edge occurs twice only when it is a graph bridge,
and the audited full-area bridge inequality makes the entire bridge population no
larger than half the metric outer detour, up to `o(D)`.

This raises the current low-`t` long-side branch from roughly `0.2074D` distinct
near-tangent contacts to more than `0.3970D` while tightening the direction window from
30 degrees to 22.5 degrees.

---

## 1. Occurrence deduplication by physical bridges

Use the outer-route notation of main:

- `d_out=b-per(F)` is total metric outer detour;
- `L_{>2}=sum_{s_i>2}(s_i-2)` is rounded long-side mass;
- for fixed `beta`, an outer-route occurrence is `beta`-good when its oriented unit
  vector lies within angle `beta` of its loaded-hull chord direction.

The exact directional-energy identity gives

\[
A_\beta\le {d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta},
\tag{BD.1}
\]

where `A_beta` is the total number of bad outer-route occurrences.  Hence the
long-side routes contain at least

\[
G_\beta
\ge
L_{>2}-{d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}
\tag{BD.2}
\]

good occurrences.

Let `B_o` be the number of graph-theoretic positive-contact bridges with both sides on
the unbounded face.

### Lemma BD.1 — exact bridge deduplication

If `E_tan(beta)` is the number of distinct physical contact edges represented among
the good long-side occurrences, then

\[
\boxed{
E_{tan}(\beta)\ge G_\beta-B_o.
}
\tag{BD.3}
\]

### Proof

In a connected plane graph, a physical edge has the same face on both sides if and
only if it is a graph bridge.  Therefore:

- a nonbridge contact edge contributes at most one occurrence to the outer-face walk;
- an outer bridge contributes exactly two outer-face occurrences.

Restricting to any subset of outer-face occurrences cannot increase either
multiplicity.  If `m_e` is the number of selected good occurrences of edge `e`, then
`m_e<=1` for nonbridges and `m_e<=2` for the `B_o` outer bridges.  Thus

\[
G_\beta=\sum_em_e
\le E_{tan}(\beta)+B_o,
\]

which is (BD.3). `square`

Combining with (BD.2),

\[
\boxed{
E_{tan}(\beta)
\ge
L_{>2}-{d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}-B_o.
}
\tag{BD.4}
\]

This is strictly stronger than the generic `G_beta/2` estimate whenever the outer
bridge population is small.

---

## 2. Full-core Type-II bridge budget

Let `U` be the union of the closures of all bounded faces of the full positive-contact
core and put `A_U=area(U)`.  The hostile-audited bridge-subtracted theorem gives the
finite inequality

\[
B_o\le {b-2\sqrt{\pi A_U}\over2}.
\tag{BD.5}
\]

In the canonical no-container full-core Type-II branch,

\[
{A_U\over D^2}\to{\pi\over4}.
\tag{BD.6}
\]

This is the audited area sandwich

\[
A_4\le A_U\le area(F),
\]

with both endpoints asymptotic to `pi D^2/4`.  Also

\[
per(F)=\pi D+o(D),
\qquad
b=per(F)+d_{out}.
\tag{BD.7}
\]

Substituting (BD.6)--(BD.7) into (BD.5) gives

\[
\boxed{
B_o\le {d_{out}\over2}+o(D).
}
\tag{BD.8}
\]

Therefore the long-side contact count sharpens to

\[
\boxed{
E_{tan}(\beta)
\ge
L_{>2}
-\left({1\over1-\cos\beta}+{1\over2}\right)d_{out}
-o(D).
}
\tag{BD.9}
\]

No KKT-weight lower bound and no two-phase hypothesis is used.  The only extra input
beyond main's directional identity is the already-audited full-core bounded-face area
saturation.

---

## 3. Current low-`t` constant with a 22.5-degree window

At the present proof-only low-`t` floor, on the long-side branch,

\[
{L_{>2}\over D}
\ge0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1),
\tag{BD.10}
\]

and

\[
{d_{out}\over D}
\le0.002883975+o(1).
\tag{BD.11}
\]

Choose the tighter direction window

\[
\boxed{\beta={\pi\over8}=22.5^\circ.}
\]

Then

\[
1-\cos(\pi/8)
=1-{\sqrt{2+\sqrt2}\over2}
=0.0761204674887132\ldots .
\]

Equation (BD.9) yields

\[
\boxed{
{E_{tan}(\pi/8)\over D}
\ge0.39706039950067\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{BD.12}
\]

Thus the long-side branch contains more than `0.397D-o(D)` distinct physical
outer-face unit contacts whose oriented directions stay within 22.5 degrees of their
corresponding long loaded-hull sides.

For comparison, using the older `beta=pi/6` window gives the still larger count

\[
{E_{tan}(\pi/6)\over D}
\ge0.41342110191459\ldots-o(1),
\tag{BD.13}
\]

rather than main's pre-bridge-deduplication value `0.2074315...`.

---

## 4. Strengthened physical low-`t` dichotomy

The companion internal-edge injectivity upgrade gives, on the tooth branch with
`eta=1/15`,

\[
{E_{int}\over D}
\ge0.3931297468701161\ldots-o(1),
\tag{BD.14}
\]

where every counted edge is an unloaded-to-unloaded internal contact crossing from a
shallow boundary layer to depth greater than `sqrt(3)/2` relative to its tooth base.

Combining (BD.12) and (BD.14), every current-floor low-`t` full-core survivor obeys

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{either}&\ge0.3931297468D-o(D)\text{ distinct internal penetration contacts},\\[1mm]
\text{or}&\ge0.3970603995D-o(D)\text{ distinct outer contacts within }22.5^\circ
\text{ of long-side tangents}.
\end{array}
}
\tag{BD.15}
\]

In particular, regardless of which side of the tooth/long-side split occurs, the
source carries at least

\[
\boxed{0.3931297468D-o(D)}
\]

distinct physical unit contacts in one of two quantitatively rigid boundary families.
This roughly doubles the branch-uniform physical-edge floor in main.

---

## 5. Remaining obstruction

The new dichotomy is still not a C-II contradiction.  Its two families live in
different parts of the positive-contact graph:

- the tooth family lies **inside unloaded components** and is suitable for the
  `e_r/r_r/s_r` component ledger;
- the long-side family lies on the **outer-face route system** and is suitable for
  train-phase or loaded-diameter/thrackle coupling.

A genuine closure now needs a common source-level payment theorem or separate
contradictions for these two families.  The numerical scarcity problem is no longer
the bottleneck: both branches force approximately `0.4D` distinct physical contacts.
